WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:06.600
Walking is not one fixed setting. A heavier load, a slippery surface, or
a slope changes what works.

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:18.376
This controller starts with central pattern generators: coupled
oscillators that organize a rhythmic gait. Eight adjustable parameters
shape the movement and its force feedback.

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:32.320
Bayesian optimization chooses what to try next. A probabilistic model
predicts how parameter settings affect a score for speed tracking and
energy use. Each trial updates that model, balancing predicted
performance against uncertainty.

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:44.357
The optimizer also uses context. Foot forces provide a load-related
estimate; body pitch provides a slope-related estimate. This helps it
learn different settings for different conditions.

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:53.563
When the target speed changes, previous trials are scored again for the
new target. Their measurements are reused instead of restarting the
search.

00:00:56.200 --> 00:01:06.461
Two feedback mechanisms help execution: contact forces adjust oscillator
timing, while virtual model control corrects body posture. Ablations
support combining them.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:19.424
On a treadmill, the Go1 learned flat-ground speed tracking in under
three minutes. Separate hardware tests demonstrated adaptation to a ten-
degree slope and added payloads up to fifteen kilograms.

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:29.347
Parameters adapt online. The search has no explicit safety guarantee;
more rugged terrain remains future work.
